WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:05.109
In a recent conversation with Vanity Fair,
Avengers: Infinity War screenwriters Christopher

00:00:05.109 --> 00:00:10.230
Markus and Stephen McFeely revealed that Smart
Hulk almost made his dramatic debut in the

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:12.940
third act of that film, during the battle
of Wakanda.

00:00:13.289 --> 00:00:17.660
The wide-ranging interview pulled back the
curtain on the massively complicated process

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:21.680
through which the two writers hammered out
the plot for the Infinity War and Endgame.

00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:26.900
Banner's transformation into Professor Hulk
was originally set to take place during Infinity

00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:31.580
War at a time in the scripting process when
the film's final act was completely different

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:33.800
from the one we eventually ended up seeing.

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:38.660
In this early version, each of the Avengers
faced a gut check before the final battle

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:42.360
in Wakanda a gut check of a very Strange nature.

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:43.480
Markus explained:

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:48.420
"There was a sequence in [Infinity War] where
[the Avengers] went into the places in the

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:53.140
Doctor Strange universe called the Mindscape,
and everyone faces themselves.

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:56.580
It was great but had absolutely nothing to
do with anything."

00:00:56.760 --> 00:01:01.460
McFeely continued to say that if this had
been kept, Hulk's entrance into the Battle

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:03.710
of Wakanda would have been pretty memorable:

00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:08.920
"The Hulk refuses to come out, if you remember,
and [in the early story line] they eventually

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:14.259
came to a realization or a compromise, and
he busted out of the Iron Man suit, and beats

00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:15.640
the heck out of this [monster]."

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:20.720
Breaking down the reason for the change, though,
McFeely pointed out that one too many victorious

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:25.960
moments during that fateful melee would have
diluted Avengers: Infinity War's march toward

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:28.000
Thanos' ultimate victory.

00:01:28.270 --> 00:01:32.660
In doing so, he also revealed that the two
movies' weird shooting schedule meant that

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:37.700
a key Endgame scene had to be re-shot once
it was determined that Professor Hulk wouldn't

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:39.220
pop up in Infinity War.

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:44.180
"That whole third act is a march toward losing,
and this Hulk scene is a big win, right?

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:49.080
It's a guy solving his problem and being a
funny character because now he's eloquent.

00:01:49.090 --> 00:01:54.280
We had to, at the last second, scrap all that,
put aside all these scenes that used to have

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:58.980
Smart Hulk, and then reshoot the first act
of Endgame... that used to have the Smart

00:01:58.980 --> 00:01:59.480
Hulk."

00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:04.160
Markus elaborated that another reason for
pushing Smart Hulk's debut until the first

00:02:04.170 --> 00:02:08.890
act of Endgame helped illustrate the changes
everyone had gone through after the five year

00:02:08.890 --> 00:02:09.380
time jump.

00:02:09.890 --> 00:02:14.660
"We wanted everybody to have this enormous
journey in the five-year jump, and to really

00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:18.380
see, in sometimes shocking ways, how the loss
had affected them.

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:22.860
We hadn’t given Banner [a change] because
we had transformed him earlier, and he had

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:23.860
nowhere to go.

00:02:23.860 --> 00:02:27.760
Suddenly, by needing to take it out of the
first movie, it was the perfect thing."

00:02:27.940 --> 00:02:32.120
"I put the brains and the brawn together,
and now look at me.

00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:34.620
The best of both worlds."

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:40.100
This helps to explain a significant mystery:
why Hulk, in all his green glory, was shown

00:02:40.100 --> 00:02:43.300
at the Battle of Wakanda in the trailers for
Infinity War.

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:47.860
Markus and McFeely confirmed that portions
of the climactic battle were shot in which

00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:53.460
Banner went into battle as the Hulk, but those
scenes also had to be removed and then re-shot.

00:02:53.650 --> 00:02:57.780
The massive Vanity Fair interview helps to
illustrate why everyone is still interested

00:02:57.790 --> 00:03:02.900
in parsing out the finer points of how Infinity
War and Endgame were made so long after the

00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:03.400
fact.

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:08.400
There are probably no two blockbusters in
existence with more moving parts, and it's

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:12.720
easy to see how things could have gone disastrously
wrong at virtually any stage.

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:18.460
Fortunately for fans of superhero cinema all
over the world, Markus, McFeely, and co-directors

00:03:18.470 --> 00:03:23.270
Joe and Anthony Russo somehow managed to make
all of those moving parts sync up as close

00:03:23.270 --> 00:03:25.000
to perfectly as seems possible.

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:30.460
It all resulted in two of the finest comic
book movies ever made, and in the case if

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:32.420
Endgame, the single biggest film of all time.

00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:37.500
Still... we would have absolutely freaked
out to see Hulk literally busting the Hulkbuster

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:38.000
suit.

00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:42.740
Unfortunately, when you're in the business
of making ultra-blockbusters, even some perfect

00:03:42.740 --> 00:03:45.820
moments have to go to make the final product
work.

